86405189 1072 #uuid "ba66d242-26a0-594d-82a7-1f167d592d18" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405189 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The rate of growth of a **car's displacement** $D(t)$ **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **time** $t$ since the car started moving.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *car's displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *interval of time* since the car started moving. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the car's displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405191 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405192 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405193 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405194 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405195 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1077 "selection" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1079 #edb/ref 17592186405191 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405190 1080 #uuid "46d988ad-b19d-531c-8043-d4cc3d8d9aff" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405191 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405191 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405191 1084 #uuid "78f2fba8-448d-54b0-9f0b-a7a12167cb79" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405192 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kD,\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405192 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405192 1084 #uuid "81d0803a-89e6-5850-b97e-24717c7d2073" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405193 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kt}{D},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405193 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405193 1084 #uuid "a3657738-9337-52ad-bf3f-63b326fd491f" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405194 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405194 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405194 1084 #uuid "60e1f64b-1691-53e6-9e99-dae3ec2ed84b" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405195 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{D},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405195 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405195 1084 #uuid "67a35e15-d65b-53a5-850a-945cbcf431a9" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405197 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1069 "q-79945" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=\\pi $, in ascending powers of $(x-\\pi )$ up to and including the term in $(x-\\pi )^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=-1.\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1072 #uuid "f2480529-9e51-5e8c-9f4c-f59546697173" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=\\pi $:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi ),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi ).$\n\nWe substitute $y(\\pi )=1$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2y{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi )$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi ) & =2y(\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =2(1)(-1)(0)+(1)^{2}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2}\\cdot (0)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-1)+… \\\\  & =1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405198 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405199 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405200 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405201 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405202 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1077 "selection" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] 